<worksheet>
<title>Factoring Trinomials - Part 1</title>
<objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this section you will see how to:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Factor a trinomial of the form <m>x^2 + bx +c</m>
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>
<p>
    Factoring trinomial (three-term) expressions is very common in mathematics. This may be the most important topic in all of Math 97. 
    If the trinomial is of the form <m>x^2 + bx +c </m>, we are looking for two numbers that add to <m>b</m> and multiply to <m>c</m>. Sometimes, we 
    may also need to first factor out a GCF. Since these are all three-term expressions, we cannot factor by grouping.
</p>
<example>
<title>Factor</title>
<statement>
    <p>
        Factor the expression <m>x^2-3x+2</m>
    </p>
</statement>
<answer>
    <p>
        <m>(x-1)(x-2)</m>
    </p>
</answer>
<solution>
    <p>
        We are looking for two numbers that add to -3 and multiply to 2. -1 and -2 are the numbers that work. The factoring is <m>(x-2)(x-1)</m>.
    </p>
</solution>
</example>
<p>
    To help determine the two numbers that will become the numbers in the factors, we can use the positive and negative signs of <m>b</m> and <m>c</m>. If both <m>b</m> 
    and <m>c</m> are both positive, then the two numbers must both be positive. If <m>b</m> is negative and <m>c</m> is positive, as in the last example, then both numbers
    must be negative. If <m>c</m> is negative, then one number must be positive and the other number must be negative.
</p>
<example>
    <title>Factor</title>
<statement>
    <p>
        Factor the expression <m>x^2+5x+6</m>
    </p>
</statement>
<answer>
    <p>
        <m>(x+2)(x+3)</m>
    </p>
</answer>
<solution>
    <p>
        The two numbers that add to 5 and multiply to 6 are 2 and 3, so the final factorization <m>(x+2)(x+3)</m>.
    </p>
</solution>
</example>
<example>
    <title>Factor</title>
<statement>
    <p>
        Factor the expression <m>x^2+x-56</m>
    </p>
</statement>
<answer>
    <p>
        <m>(x+8)(x-7)</m>
    </p>
</answer>
<solution>
    <p>
        The two numbers that add to -1 and multiply to 56 are 8 and -7, so the final factorization <m>(x+8)(x-7)</m>.
    </p>
</solution>
</example>
<example>
    <title>Factor</title>
<statement>
    <p>
        Factor the expression <m>x^2-11x+18</m>
    </p>
</statement>
<answer>
    <p>
        <m>(x-9)(x-2)</m>
    </p>
</answer>
<solution>
    <p>
        The two numbers that add to -11 and multiply to 18 are -9 and -2, so the final factorization <m>(x-9)(x-2)</m>.
    </p>
</solution>
</example>
<example>
    <title>Factor</title>
<statement>
    <p>
        Factor the expression <m>x^2-2x-48</m>
    </p>
</statement>
<answer>
    <p>
        <m>(x-8)(x+6)</m>
    </p>
</answer>
<solution>
    <p>
        The two numbers that add to -2 and multiply to -48 are -8 and 6, so the final factorization <m>(x-8)(x+6)</m>.
    </p>
</solution>
</example>
<p>
    Sometimes we must first factor out a GCF. Do not lose the GCF when you factor that trinomial!
</p>
<example>
    <title>Factor</title>
<statement>
    <p>
        Factor the expression <m>4x^2+24x-28</m>
    </p>
</statement>
<answer>
    <p>
        <m>4(x+7)(x-1)</m>
    </p>
</answer>
<solution>
    <p>
        First factor out the GCF of 4 giving <m>4(x^2-6x+7)</m>. Now we are looking for two numbers that add to -6 and multiply 7. The numbers are 7 and -1, 
        so the final factorization <m>4(x+7)(x-1)</m>.
    </p>
</solution>
</example>
<p>
    <alert>Try it Now!</alert>
</p>
<exercise>
